WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.035
-We have something in common.
We were discussing backstage.

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:05.371
Both of our mothers --
French teachers.

00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:07.207
-Yes.
-Yeah. Did that mean --

00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:09.174
Does that mean you're good
at it? Do you know it?

00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:12.178
-No. I was for a while.
-Yeah.

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:13.679
-How about you?
-Never.

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:15.414
And she was my French teacher.

00:00:15.414 --> 00:00:16.716
Was she your actual --
-Yeah.

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:18.417
Well, I had a French teacher
in school,

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:19.919
and then my mom
taught me at home.

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:23.589
-Oh, that's a passive-aggressive
comment on the one at school.

00:00:23.589 --> 00:00:25.925
-I was -- You know what?

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:27.293
I think I was good for a while,

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:28.794
but, then, I haven't
spoken it in 20 years.

00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:30.162
I kind of understand
a little bit.

00:00:30.162 --> 00:00:32.264
-Yeah. I always feel it's
dangerous when you tell people

00:00:32.264 --> 00:00:34.300
your mom's a French teacher
because then they just jump

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.102
to the assumption
that you listened to your mother

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:37.470
and now know French.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.737
-Immediately.
-Immediately.

00:00:38.737 --> 00:00:40.340
And you're like,
"Oh, no, no, no! I'm a bad kid!"

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:41.741
-Yeah.

00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:43.743
-You have two sons.
-That's right.

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:47.046
-And they were huge fans of
the first "Quiet Place" film.

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:50.950
-Yeah, so, we went
to see it in the cinema.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.985
I suppose it's two years ago,
the first one came out.

00:00:52.985 --> 00:00:54.420
-Yeah.
I think that's about right.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.754
-Yeah, the three of us.
Just the three of us.

00:00:55.754 --> 00:00:58.691
And I didn't really know
that much about the movie.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.026
I knew it was good.

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:01.494
But I said,
"Come on, guys, we'll go."

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:04.263
And so we watched it,
and, you know, they had --

00:01:04.263 --> 00:01:06.999
I think they had these hoodies,
so by the end of it,

00:01:06.999 --> 00:01:10.770
they were just sat with the
hoodies like this, watching it.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.372
And, you know,

00:01:13.372 --> 00:01:14.907
it's a magnificent achievement,
the first movie.

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:17.042
And for me, the thing
that I loved so much about it

00:01:17.042 --> 00:01:20.012
was the emotion.
-Sure.

00:01:20.012 --> 00:01:22.214
-It just hit me emotionally,
you know?

00:01:22.214 --> 00:01:25.184
-I wonder, as a dad -- I think
it will hit me emotionally

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:29.722
when I'm with my kids at a film
that is affecting them that way.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:31.724
Like, being scared,
I remember as a kid,

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:33.759
being such an exciting
thing in the cinema.

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:35.862
It must have been so fun
to be with your kids

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:37.464
and watching how movies
affect them.

00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:39.765
-Yes, it was,
and because, essentially,

00:01:39.765 --> 00:01:43.303
it's a film about family,
as is the sequel, you know?

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:45.604
And it's a film that insists

00:01:45.604 --> 00:01:48.007
On being watched in a cinema
with others

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:50.310
'cause it's
a collective experience.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.045
You feel that anxiety
and that jeopardy

00:01:53.045 --> 00:01:55.381
and that tension with
everybody else in the cinema,

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:58.984
which is kind of what
it was made for, cinema.

00:01:58.984 --> 00:02:02.255
-Are they then very excited that
their dad is in the sequel?

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:03.989
-Well, how will I put this?

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:09.195
Like, I'm not cool,
but I'm not uncool.

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:10.730
So I'm like --

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.899
-You just found that
soft middle of dadhood?

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:15.668
-Yeah, I'm like, "Well, okay."

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:17.203
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:19.171
-'Cause, you know,
it doesn't strike --

00:02:19.171 --> 00:02:20.573
I'm a huge fan of your films.

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:22.041
There's not a lot
of kid-friendly work.

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:23.843
Like, it must be nice for them

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:26.212
to finally have something
they can see you in.

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:27.647
-No, I was like --

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:31.084
It's only now that some stuff
is not highly inappropriate.

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:34.086
-Yeah, 'cause I've never heard
someone say, "Oh, you have kids?

00:02:34.086 --> 00:02:36.188
You've got to watch
'Peaky Blinders.'"

00:02:36.188 --> 00:02:37.657
-[ Laughs ] Yeah. No.

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:38.925
-Yeah.
-No.

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:42.228
-That must be a weird show
to be a part of

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:44.464
because this started
as a very small production,

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.132
"Peaky Blinders."
-Yes. Yeah.

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:48.434
-This was not one of those
"Game of Thrones" type shows

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.069
that was aiming for the stars.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:53.606
It just sort of built a very
large international following.

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:55.107
-Yeah.

00:02:55.107 --> 00:02:56.809
-Has it been a trip to be a part
of something like that?

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.110
-Oh, for sure. You know,

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.013
it started as a little show
on BBC Two in the UK.

00:03:01.013 --> 00:03:04.717
And then very -- 'Cause
it had no advertising budget.

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:06.819
The BBC don't generally
advertise.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:09.388
So it just --
It was word of mouth, you know?

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:10.856
Just word of mouth.

00:03:10.856 --> 00:03:12.759
And gradually --
We've been doing it now --

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:15.294
We've been shooting it for
eight years or something,

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:17.363
and we're about to start
series six,

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:20.999
so, yeah, it's crazy how many
people across the world

00:03:20.999 --> 00:03:25.070
and how obsessive the fan --
and loyal the fans are.

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:26.939
-And it's --
-"Loyal."

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:28.974
-Loyal. Yeah, yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:28.974 --> 00:03:31.510
Uh, and I hope --

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.580
I hope this comment will not
run afoul of your fan base,

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.483
but the show had to be very good
for people to get over

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:39.652
how bad a title
"Peaky Blinders" is.

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.754
-[ Laughs ]
-You thought this, as well.

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:44.023
-I mean, I was confused.

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:46.625
-Yeah, it's a very confusing --

00:03:46.625 --> 00:03:49.762
"Peaky Blinders" seems like
the name of a cartoon horse.

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:51.697
[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:54.099
And if you haven't seen
the show, it's not that.

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:57.236
-No. There's some horses in it.
-Yeah, there are some horses.

00:03:57.236 --> 00:04:01.840
And the -- One of the many great
things about "Peaky Blinders"

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.643
is the haircuts.
-Yeah.

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:07.279
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:10.149
-And, you know, you obviously
have to then have that haircut.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.650
Like, that's not --

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:13.619
-Yeah, that's coming in
about ten days' time.

00:04:13.619 --> 00:04:16.555
-Yeah. No wonder your kids,
like, have issues.

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:19.625
I'm just saying, they can't
think you're that cool

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:22.795
when you're, like, the one dad
walking around with your sides.

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:26.865
-Well, yeah, that's why they
wear hoodies, and I wear --

00:04:26.865 --> 00:04:28.634
-They try to get you
to wear a hoodie.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:31.170
-Yeah, I -- You know, the
mad thing about that haircut,

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.140
as well, it was designed I think
as a sort of a protection

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.943
against lice and infestation.

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:39.245
And now all the hipsters
are wearing it.

00:04:39.245 --> 00:04:42.515
-Yeah. Does that mean
lice is making a comeback?

00:04:42.515 --> 00:04:44.283
[ Laughs ] Might be.
-Who knows?

00:04:44.283 --> 00:04:46.118
-That would be the least
of our problems right now.

00:04:46.118 --> 00:04:47.453
-That's true.

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:50.055
-You were in the Christopher
Nolan "Dark Knight" films

00:04:50.055 --> 00:04:52.224
playing the Scarecrow.

00:04:52.224 --> 00:04:54.493
I did not realize --
you auditioned to play Batman.

00:04:54.493 --> 00:04:58.264
-Well, yes, amongst
many other actors, yeah, yeah.

00:04:58.264 --> 00:05:01.100
-Yeah, did you have a sense --

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.902
I mean, obviously,
Christopher Nolan

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:05.505
still was a fan of yours
because he put you in the films.

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:07.039
Did you at any point think like,

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:08.741
"Oh, I'm a perfect fit
for this"? Or did you --

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:10.009
When you were
in the Batman suit,

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:11.510
did you think --
What were your thoughts?

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:12.845
'Cause I imagine
that's intimidating.

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:15.982
-No, I never for a moment
thought I was material.

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:17.082
I think I was --

00:05:17.082 --> 00:05:18.651
It was obvious

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:20.386
that Christian Bale
was going to be Batman.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.054
At that time, it was clear,

00:05:22.054 --> 00:05:24.323
and he's such a magnificent
Batman and Bruce Wayne.

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:27.726
But I did get to -- I think
it was the Val Kilmer suit.

00:05:27.726 --> 00:05:32.865
I got to try it on, which
they had to adjust slightly.

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:34.533
But it's a weird thing.

00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:37.403
You put it on,
you put the cowl on,

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:38.937
and your voi--

00:05:38.937 --> 00:05:40.306
Just, the voice drops.

00:05:40.306 --> 00:05:42.641
-Yeah, sure.
-It drops a couple of octaves.

00:05:42.641 --> 00:05:45.444
You can't do the Batman voice
without doing the...

00:05:45.444 --> 00:05:47.413
[Deep voice] Batman.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:49.314
That's why I couldn't do it
because I would be like,

00:05:49.314 --> 00:05:50.482
"I'm Batman!"
They'd be like,

00:05:50.482 --> 00:05:52.351
"No, you're not!
Get out of here!

00:05:52.351 --> 00:05:54.186
Your just a guy in a suit!"

00:05:54.186 --> 00:05:57.256
Hey. Congrats on the film.
Congrats on "Peaky Blinders."

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.825
That's so exciting that
we have another season coming.

00:05:59.825 --> 00:06:02.695
Thanks so much for being here.
That's Cillian Murphy.

